      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "Quantum key distribution made quantum measurement and entanglement relevant to a concrete information-security task and helped establish quantum information as an experimental field.",
        "attribution": "Bennett and Brassard developed the influential protocol; Ekert introduced a distinct entanglement-based approach. The three names capture central conceptual advances.",
        "maturity": "Protocols have been implemented over fiber and satellite links, though practical deployments still face engineering limits and do not replace all cryptographic infrastructure.",
        "uncertainties": [
          "The field's impact is clear, but the distinction between foundational physics and subsequent communication engineering needs careful framing."
        ]
      }
    },
